window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"17-30","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"17-30-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-01/","title_he":"א – כשרות החלב","title_en":"The Kashrut of Milk","body_en":"<p>Pure milk is milk that was milked from a pure (kosher) domesticated or wild animal, that is, from species of domesticated and wild animals whose meat is kosher to eat, such as a cow, a goat and a deer. Impure milk is milk that was milked from an impure domesticated or wild animal, whose meat is forbidden to eat, such as a she-donkey, a female camel, a mare and a sow. This is the rule: \"That which comes from the impure is impure, and that which comes from the pure is pure\" (Bekhorot 5b).<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>There are two differences between pure and impure milk: a) Pure milk can curdle into cheese, whereas impure milk does not curdle. b) The color of pure milk is white, and that of impure milk is yellowish (Avoda Zara 35b).</p>\n<p>Even when the milk is pure, it may still be forbidden, for if it was milked from a tereifa animal, it is forbidden to eat. A tereifa is an animal that was injured in a way that it will not live more than twelve months, or that has another defect that will cause its death within twelve months (above 20:2). Since death is eating away at its flesh, even if it is slaughtered with a kosher sheḥita, its meat is forbidden to eat. And since its meat is forbidden to eat, its milk is also forbidden to eat. If a tereifa animal became mixed in with kosher animals and was milked together with the other animals, then if the kosher animals were sixty times the tereifa, the milk of the tereifa is nullified in sixty and all the milk milked from the herd is kosher; and if they were not sixty times as many, all the milk is forbidden (SA YD 81:1-2).</p>\n<p>If a cow was milked and afterwards slaughtered, and it turned out to be a tereifa, just as its meat is forbidden to eat, so the milk that was milked from it is forbidden to eat, and the cheese made from that milk is also forbidden to eat. But if it is possible that the defect that rendered it tereifa arose after the milk from which the cheese was made was milked from it, the cheese is kosher, for as long as there is no proof that the animal was a tereifa at the time of milking, its milk retains the presumptive status of being kosher (ibid.).</p>\n<p>When an animal became tereifa because of a lung adhesion (sirkha), the milk that was milked from it in the three days closest to its slaughter is forbidden. However, milk that was milked from it before then is permitted because of a 'double doubt' (sfek sfeika): perhaps the adhesion formed after the milk was milked, and perhaps this adhesion does not render it tereifa (SA and Rema YD 81:2; Zivḥei Tzedek 18; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 27).<sup>2</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"The Talmud (Bekhorot 6b) asks why it is permitted to drink milk, since only after the slaughter of a domesticated or wild animal is it permitted to eat its meat, whereas before that the whole animal is forbidden, and anything separated from it is forbidden because of 'a limb from a living animal' (ever min ha-ḥai) or 'meat from a living animal' (basar min ha-ḥai). Not only that, but milk is blood that changed, became turbid and turned into milk, and since blood is forbidden to eat, there would be reason to think that milk is also forbidden to eat. The Sages explained that since we learned in the Torah that the praise of the Land of Israel is that it is \"a land flowing with milk and honey,\" it is clear that milk and honey are kosher to eat."},{"n":2,"en":"At first glance this is difficult: among dairy cows there are cows that are tereifot because of lung adhesions at the rate of a 'common minority' (mi'ut ha-matzui), and therefore by rabbinic law they must be checked after their slaughter (above 20:4). How, then, can the milk of a cow be permitted when it is known that a 'common minority' of cows are tereifa? Rashi (Responsa 60) explained that the whole obligation to check in the case of a 'common minority' applies when it is possible to check, but when there is no possibility of checking, as with a cow before slaughter, we rely on the basic Torah law of following the majority. And so the Rishonim and Aḥaronim agreed. The Mishkenot Ya'akov (YD 17) explained another important principle: the rule that a living animal has the presumptive status of being forbidden applies only to its meat, but regarding its milk, as long as we have not seen that the cow became tereifa, the milk by its nature has the presumptive status of being permitted (and similarly in Shibolei Ha-leket 2:27). And although in practice many cows are found to be tereifot after slaughter, as long as they have not been slaughtered, the milk of each and every cow has the presumptive status of being kosher, since no grounds for suspicion (re'uta) have arisen regarding the cow from which it was milked.<br>Yet one may still ask: sometimes the majority of dairy cows are found to be tereifot, and if so we ought to be concerned about drinking the milk on the basis of the majority. This question was asked regarding the permission to slaughter on Yom Tov (above 20:8, note 10): how is it permitted to slaughter in places and times where most animals are declared tereifa, so that most likely the slaughter is not for the purpose of eating, since the animal will be found to be tereifa? The poskim answered that in truth, according to the basic law, a significant majority of the animals are kosher, and only because of stringencies regarding adhesions do we declare many tereifa (Pri Ḥadash 39:3; Sha'agat Aryeh 64; Shiyurei Berakha 81:6; Rabbi Akiva Eiger; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 39:22). It follows that the permission of the milk according to Rashi's reasoning, which rests on the majority, also stands. And according to the reasoning of the Mishkenot Ya'akov, that the milk has a presumptive status of being kosher, even if it were clear after slaughter that most of the animals are tereifa by Torah law, the milk of each and every cow has a presumptive status of being kosher as long as it has not been proven to be a tereifa (Tiferet Tzvi YD 13).<br>Another question has arisen in recent generations: sometimes veterinarians need to operate on cows in places where, according to many, the incision renders the animal tereifa, and consequently their milk becomes forbidden; and since they are generally not separated from the other cows, if there are not sixty times as many kosher cows there, should not all the milk of the herd be forbidden to eat? Answer: First, some hold that any operation performed by a veterinarian for the sake of the cow's health, after which it is known that it will be able to live more than twelve months, does not render it tereifa, and so it appears in practice (above 20:3). Second, most operations are performed in places where there is a dispute whether an incision there renders it tereifa, such as a perforation of the rumen, and consequently it is a double doubt (above, ibid.). In addition, there is no complete certainty that milk brought from a particular dairy barn comes from a barn that has a cow that underwent an operation. And even if it is clear that there is a cow there that underwent an operation, perhaps its milk is nullified in sixty. And even if it is clear that there is not sixty times as much, it is the milk of a possible tereifa, and the entire law of nullification in sixty for a substance mixed with its own kind is rabbinic, and in a case of doubt the halakha follows the lenient view (as the Ḥokhmat Adam wrote, 37:6). Several Aḥaronim further wrote that if it is known that in the natural course these cows live twelve months after the operation, one may even ab initio nullify their milk in the milk of the majority of cows that were not operated on (Har Tzvi YD 36; Tzitz Eliezer 20:29; and see Yabi'a Omer vol. 8, YD 2; unlike Responsa Maharsham 3:129, who was stringent). And although according to halakha and in practice animals that were operated on are kosher (above 20:3), since many are stringent and consider them tereifot, in 'mehadrin' kashrut one must be careful not to mix in milk from cows that were operated on in places where there is a dispute whether this renders them tereifa."}]},{"slug":"17-30-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-02/","title_he":"ב – איסור חלב נוכרים","title_en":"The Prohibition of Milk of Non-Jews (Ḥalav Akum)","body_en":"<p>The Sages forbade Jews to eat milk that a non-Jew milked, lest he mixed impure milk into the pure milk (Avoda Zara 35b). This is despite the fact that the likelihood of this is very small, since the overwhelming majority of the milk that people usually drink and eat is pure milk. Moreover, there is a difference between impure and pure milk, for the color of pure milk is white and the color of impure milk is yellowish; consequently, if the milk is white and no different taste is detected in it, then even if the non-Jew mixed impure milk into it, by Torah law it is nullified in the majority of pure milk. Nevertheless, the Sages were very stringent and forbade the milk of a non-Jew lest he mixed impure milk into it. Not only that, but even if a Jew made cheese from a non-Jew's milk, which would seem to prove that the milk is pure, since the milk of an impure animal does not curdle, the Sages forbade the cheese, out of concern that impure milk that did not curdle remained as moisture in the cavities and crevices of the cheese. Apparently, the Sages were more stringent about this concern of prohibition than is customary with other food prohibitions because of the general aim of distancing Jews from the foods of non-Jews.<sup>3</sup></p>\n<p>However, when a Jew supervises the non-Jew so that he does not mix impure milk into the pure milk, the milk is kosher. If it is known with certainty that the non-Jew has no impure animal, it is sufficient that the Jew watches that the non-Jew does not bring milk from elsewhere in the middle of the milking. If the non-Jew has an impure animal, the Jew must watch that he milks the pure animal and does not mix milk of the impure animal into its milk. He need not see the entire milking; rather, it is sufficient that the non-Jew knows that the Jew is supervising him and could easily see him, for example, if he stood up he would see him milking, or he could come in and see him at any moment (Avoda Zara 39b; SA 115:1). When there is a concern that impure milk remained in the non-Jew's vessel from a previous milking, the Jew must check that the vessel is clean of milk residue (Rema and commentators there).</p>","notes_en":[{"n":3,"en":"Milk of non-Jews is forbidden by rabbinic law, for by Torah law, since most available milk is from a pure animal, we follow the majority and are not concerned that the non-Jew mixed impure milk into it (Issur Ve-heter He-arokh 45:7). And even if he mixed in impure milk, as long as its taste is not detectable, by Torah law it is nullified (see Shakh 118:8; Dagul Me-revava there; Pitḥei Teshuva 1).<br>In the Babylonian Talmud, Avoda Zara 35b, it is explained that the reason for the prohibition is the concern that impure milk was mixed in. In the Yerushalmi, Avoda Zara 2:8, the opinion of R. Yehoshua ben Levi is also brought, that the prohibition is because of uncovered liquids (giluy, the concern of snake venom). The Rishonim wrote the reason brought in the Bavli. R. Shmuel Abuhav (Sefer Ha-zikhronot 3:3) added that the general aim of the prohibition is to distance us from the foods of non-Jews. In any case, it is agreed that regarding the prohibition of milk of non-Jews, the Sages were concerned about possibilities more remote than is customary for other prohibitions. Therefore, when a secular Jew milked the cows, as long as there is no reasonable concern that he will mix in impure milk, the milk is kosher (Igrot Moshe YD 1:46; 2:47; Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv, Kovetz Teshuvot 3:115; unlike Levushei Mordekhai YD Tinyana 56).<br>The concern is at the time of milking, but once the milk has been milked in a kosher manner, the law of guarding it is like the law of guarding other foods. Milk of a non-Jew is considered a definite prohibition and not a doubtful prohibition, even though it was forbidden because of the concern that impure milk was mixed into it (Torat Ḥatat 43:7). If it became mixed into pure milk, some say that since it is the same kind and its taste is not so perceptible, it is nullified in a majority (see Pri Megadim 97, Siftei Da'at 1). And some say that it is nullified in sixty (Issur Ve-heter 47:7; Dagul Me-revava 115:9; Zivḥei Tzedek 23)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-03/","title_he":"ג – חלב נוכרים כשאין חשש חלב טמא","title_en":"Milk of Non-Jews When There Is No Concern of Impure Milk","body_en":"<p>Three views have been stated regarding the scope of the prohibition. According to the first view, only when there is a realistic concern that the non-Jew will mix impure milk into the pure milk is milk milked by a non-Jew forbidden. But in a place where impure animals are not raised, or where the milk of an impure animal is much more expensive, there is no concern that the non-Jew will mix impure milk into pure, and a Jew may eat milk that a non-Jew milked (Tashbetz, Rashbash, Pri Ḥadash). And so was the custom in most communities of North Africa and Yemen.</p>\n<p>According to the second view, even in a place where there is no realistic concern that the non-Jew will mix impure milk into pure, as long as there is any concern whatsoever, even an extremely remote one, a Jew may not eat milk that a non-Jew milked. In practice, since impure animals were sometimes brought from place to place, and sometimes the non-Jew thought he would benefit from mixing impure milk into the pure milk, in order to preserve it for a long time or to improve its taste, they forbade all milk milked by a non-Jew. Only when the non-Jew milked the pure animals in a place where there is no concern at all that impure milk will be brought to him is the milk kosher. And so was the custom in many communities in the regions around the Land of Israel and in Ashkenaz (Ḥida, Beit Meir, Ḥokhmat Adam).</p>\n<p>According to the third view, even when there is no concern at all that the non-Jew will mix impure milk into pure, the milk milked by the non-Jew is forbidden. This is because, on the basis of the concern that the non-Jew might mix impure milk into pure, the Sages decreed by a vote (be-minyan), that is, by a definitive decision, a complete prohibition on the milk of non-Jews, even when there is no concern at all that impure milk is mixed into it (Ḥatam Sofer, Ḥelkat Ya'akov).<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"It is generally customary to arrange this topic around the dispute over whether the prohibition of milk of non-Jews was enacted 'by a vote,' that is, by a definitive decision, and applies even when there is no concern at all that impure milk was mixed in. But in truth there are many opinions on the topic, which can be divided into three views. <b>The first view</b> divides into several opinions. The lenient authorities hold that when a non-Jew has no impure animal in his herd, the milk he milks is kosher (some cited in the Mordekhai; Tashbetz 3:143). Some say that only if there is no impure animal in the entire town, or impure milk is more expensive, is the milk that the non-Jew milks kosher (Pri Ḥadash 115:6; Ḥazon Ish YD 41:4). And some say that this is on condition that the impure milk is several times more expensive (Pri To'ar 115:1). And so was the custom in most communities of North Africa (Otzar Ha-mikhtavim 3, §1392). <b>The second view</b>: the prohibition applies even when the concern is very remote, and so was the custom in the Land of Israel and in Turkey (Ḥida, Birkei Yosef 115:1). And so wrote the Ḥokhmat Adam (67:1): \"For anything the Sages decreed, if it exists in reality, even remotely, it is nevertheless forbidden.\" And so wrote the Beit Meir, Avnei Nezer (YD 101), Arukh Ha-shulḥan 115:16, and others (and it is doubtful whether the Radbaz 4:75 belongs to the first view or the second). <b>The third view:</b> this prohibition was decreed by a vote on all milk that a non-Jew milked, even when there is no concern at all that it contains impure milk. So wrote the Ḥatam Sofer YD 107 and Melamed Le-ho'il 36:4, and R. Shmuel Abuhav preceded them in Sefer Ha-zikhronot. According to the Ḥatam Sofer, even if the conclusion of the Talmudic discussion is like the Pri Ḥadash, since in Ashkenaz the custom was to be stringent, this is binding like a vow; and so wrote the Ḥelkat Ya'akov YD 34 and Minḥat Yitzḥak 9:25."}]},{"slug":"17-30-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-04/","title_he":"ד – המחלוקת על חלב מחברות גדולות בימינו","title_en":"The Dispute about Milk from Large Companies Today","body_en":"<p>Many hold that since in practice there is no concern that the large companies that market milk and its products will mix impure milk into their milk, because impure animals are not milked for them at all, and the price of impure milk is incomparably higher than that of pure milk, and there is also no benefit in mixing it into pure milk, according to most poskim (the first and second views in the previous halakha) their milk is not subject to the prohibition of 'milk of non-Jews.' Rav Moshe Feinstein added (Igrot Moshe YD 1:47-49) that even according to the stringent view, one may be lenient regarding dairy products of reliable, supervised companies in well-ordered countries that declare that the milk and its products they market are the milk of pure animals. This is because if they deceive, they are liable to be fined, and no less seriously, consumer trust in their products is liable to be harmed, and they will lose many customers. It follows that the general supervision in these countries is considered like the supervision of a Jew who watches that impure milk is not mixed into the pure milk, and consequently, according to all views, their milk is permitted. Therefore, taking into account the difficulty of obtaining 'ḥalav Yisrael' (milk milked under Jewish supervision) outside Israel, Rav Feinstein permitted in the USA relying on the reliability of supervised companies. And this is the practice of the widespread kashrut system in the USA, the OU.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, some are stringent, for two main reasons: a) Perhaps the government supervision is not tight enough, and even if it is tight, people might learn from this to be lenient in places that are not properly supervised. b) According to the view that the prohibition of milk of non-Jews is a complete prohibition enacted by a vote (the third view in the previous halakha), even if the government supervision is tight, it is not considered like the supervision of a Jew that the Sages permitted, and consequently the milk is forbidden (Ḥelkat Ya'akov YD 34; Mishneh Halakhot 4:103).</p>\n<p>In practice, the main ruling follows the lenient opinion; however, the holy people of Israel are accustomed to be scrupulous when this does not involve great difficulty, as explained below (in halakha 6).</p>\n<p>Today it is possible to supervise the milking by electronic means, and when this supervision is managed properly, the milk is kosher le-mehadrin according to all views.<sup>5</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"Today it is possible to supervise the dairies of non-Jews with the help of cameras that transmit everything happening in the dairy live to a control room, and these recordings are kept for several days, so that the milkers can be supervised even after the fact. Supervision of this kind is a greater deterrent than supervision by a Jew who can look at the milkers, and therefore milk milked under such supervision is kosher le-mehadrin. Even so, some rabbis cast doubt on this with various arguments: perhaps the supervisor will be lazy and not look at the recordings; perhaps the cameras will not cover the entire milking area and the milk tanks; perhaps the cameras will break down; and perhaps the recordings will be falsified. However, it appears that when a serious kashrut system is responsible for this, one may rely on it to manage the supervision properly, and the milk milked under its supervision is kosher le-mehadrin. In recent years the ability to supervise has improved further, for all advanced dairies use computers that recognize each and every cow and track and record its milk quantity every day, so that there is no concern that other milk will enter the system. And so wrote Rav Weitman in Teḥumin 22. And so ruled Rav Eliyahu in Ma'amar Mordekhai 1, YD 4:4, and so instructed Rav Yosef Shalom Elyashiv and Rav Wosner."}]},{"slug":"17-30-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-05/","title_he":"ה – אבקת חלב נוכרים","title_en":"Powdered Milk of Non-Jews","body_en":"<p>Another dispute arose regarding 'powdered milk,' that is, milk whose liquids have been evaporated until only a powder remains of it. 'Powdered milk' is used to flavor products such as chocolate, when the concentrated substances and flavors of milk are wanted without the added volume of the liquids. Also, 'powdered milk' can be stored for a long time, and when needed, water can be added to it to obtain a drink similar to regular milk, containing almost all its nutritional components.</p>\n<p>According to most poskim, since it is clear to us that the 'powdered milk' was made from pure milk, according to the first and second views we learned in the previous halakhot, it is kosher. In addition, in well-ordered countries where the companies are supervised so that they do not deceive and introduce impure milk into the cow's milk from which 'powdered milk' is produced, the governmental and public supervision can be regarded as the supervision of a Jew, and then even according to the third view the milk would be kosher. And even one who does not agree to be lenient on the basis of these arguments regarding the milk of a non-Jew should be lenient regarding 'powdered milk,' because the Sages decreed a prohibition on milk milked by a non-Jew and not on a new product made from it. Just as they needed to enact an additional decree regarding cheese, and without it cheese would have been permitted, so too, without a special decree on 'powdered milk,' when it is clear to us that it was made from the milk of a pure animal, it is permitted to eat.</p>\n<p>Some of those who are stringent regarding a non-Jew's milk milked for a reliable company are also stringent regarding 'powdered milk' made from it, because 'powdered milk' is the milk itself without its liquids, and consequently the decree on the milk of a non-Jew also applies to 'powdered milk' produced from it. And the reason a special decree had to be enacted regarding cheese is that cheese is always produced from pure milk, since only pure milk curdles, and consequently, when they wished to forbid the cheese of non-Jews, they had to forbid it by an additional decree for other reasons. But 'powdered milk' can also be made from impure milk, and consequently the decree on milk applies to it as well.<sup>6</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"The lenient authorities: Zaken Aharon vol. 2, YD 44; Har Tzvi YD 103; Devar Yehoshua vol. 3, YD 17-19; Beit Avi vol. 1, YD 72; and others. And so agreed: Yaskil Avdi vol. 5, YD 9; Tzitz Eliezer 16:25. On the other hand, the stringent authorities: Rav Mordekhai Eliyahu in Ma'amar Mordekhai vol. 1, YD 4; Binyan Av 5:47. And the Shevet Ha-levi 5:59 inclined to this, and was lenient only in a time of need. Powdered milk has the status of a dairy food, and if it became mixed into another food, even though it has been drained of its liquids, it is nullified in sixty and the food is pareve. Only if the taste of milk is actually perceptible in the food should it be considered dairy (see Shevet Ha-kehati 4:195)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-06/","title_he":"ו – הלכה למעשה במוצרי חלב","title_en":"Practical Halakha Regarding Dairy Products","body_en":"<p>In the fundamental dispute about milk and 'powdered milk' from reliable, supervised companies, the halakha follows the lenient opinion, because their reasoning is convincing, and the opinion of most of the Rishonim and Aḥaronim is to be lenient; and in addition, the rule is that in a dispute concerning rabbinic law, the halakha follows the lenient opinion. Regarding 'powdered milk,' the position of those who permit is even broader, since even among those who are stringent regarding a non-Jew's milk there are some who are lenient regarding 'powdered milk.' Therefore, regular kashrut certification may be given to dairy products known not to contain impure milk, even though they were milked without Jewish supervision. However, mehadrin kashrut should be given only to milk milked under Jewish supervision, by a supervisor or by cameras. And the holy people of Israel, as long as the stringency does not involve high costs, are accustomed to be scrupulous in accordance with all the poskim. Therefore, in all the certifications of the rabbinates in the Land of Israel it is accepted practice to be scrupulous not to use milk of non-Jews, but products containing powdered milk are given certification according to the basic halakha.</p>\n<p>Mehadrin kashrut requires care regarding two more issues. The first: as we learned (above 20:3), veterinarians sometimes have to perform operations on animals, such as a Caesarean section on an animal having difficulty giving birth, and perforating the rumen to release dangerous gases. Some say that after these operations they are considered tereifot, and consequently their milk becomes forbidden to eat. And although we learned (above 20, note 4) that the main halakhic ruling follows the lenient authorities, who hold that these operations do not render the cows tereifa, and in addition, the milk of the operated cows is nullified in the milk of the kosher ones, in mehadrin kashrut one should take into account the stringent opinion and not mix in milk from cows that underwent such operations.</p>\n<p>The second: in mehadrin kashrut one must be careful not to mix in milk milked in dairy barns where Shabbat observance is not maintained. And indeed, as we will learn (in halakha 11), since the prohibition of benefiting from milk milked in desecration of Shabbat is rabbinic, in a situation of doubt there is no prohibition. Therefore, in regular kashrut it is possible to include milk brought from dairy barns where Shabbat observance is not maintained, since there is no certainty that it is milk milked in desecration of Shabbat, for perhaps it was milked on a weekday and perhaps it was milked on Shabbat by a non-Jew. But in mehadrin kashrut one must be careful to use milk from dairy barns where Shabbat observance is maintained.</p>\n<p>In summary, kashrut certification may be given to milk of non-Jews, and to 'powdered milk' made from milk of non-Jews, that was milked for large, supervised companies, without concern about milk of tereifot. Likewise, kashrut certification may be given to milk from Jewish dairy barns where they are not careful to milk without desecrating Shabbat. And so various kashrut bodies practice. Those who wish to be scrupulous are stringent on these four issues, and this is the definition of dairy products that are kosher le-mehadrin.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-30-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-07/","title_he":"ז – איסור גבינת גויים","title_en":"The Prohibition of Cheese of Non-Jews (Gevinat Akum)","body_en":"<p>In addition to the prohibition of milk of non-Jews, the Sages also forbade the cheese of non-Jews; that is, even if the milk is kosher, for example, because the non-Jew bought it from a Jew or because there is no impure animal anywhere in the area, the Sages decreed that the cheese he prepared is forbidden to eat. The Tanna'im and Amora'im gave many reasons for the decree, and from the fact that it was not clear what the reason for the decree was, one can learn that the general aim of the Sages in the decree on cheese, as in additional decrees on the foods of non-Jews, was to separate between Jews and non-Jews. In practice, however, they based their decree on the concern of a specific prohibition, upon which they established the sweeping prohibition on all cheeses. In halakha it was agreed that the main reason on which the prohibition of the cheese of non-Jews was founded is that non-Jews were accustomed to curdle the cheese with the stomach lining of a nevela (an animal not slaughtered properly) (Avoda Zara 29b; 35a-b).</p>\n<p>To produce cheese from milk, one must mix into the milk enzymes (rennet) that break down the milk, separate the liquids and sugars from the proteins and fats, and bind the proteins and fats into a single mass, which is the cheese. Naturally, these enzymes are secreted in the stomach of mammals, to help them digest the milk they suckle. The food being digested in the stomach is called 'kiva' (rennet), whereas the stomach itself is called 'the stomach lining' (or ha-kiva). Jews would prepare cheeses using the 'kiva' of pure animals; that is, they used the contents of the stomach, in which these enzymes were present, to curdle the milk. Non-Jews, however, also used pieces of the stomach lining to curdle the milk. And since they did not slaughter the animals according to halakha, this lining was forbidden as nevela, and the cheeses curdled with it are forbidden to eat. And although the milk is much more than sixty times the stomach lining, since the cheese is curdled by it, the prohibition of nevela is not nullified in sixty, as its effect is evident in the cheese.</p>\n<p>In recent generations the food industry has advanced, and today these enzymes are prepared from microorganisms. Still, there are places where stomach lining is used to make cheeses; this is done in less developed places, and it is done in developed countries when special cheeses with a tradition of generations are made.</p>\n<p>The halakhic ruling is that even when it is known with certainty that the cheese that non-Jews prepared was made with enzymes that contain no prohibition, from plants or from microorganisms, their cheese is forbidden. This is because the decree of the Sages was enacted in a sweeping manner on all cheeses prepared by non-Jews.<sup>7</sup></p>\n<p>The poskim disagree whether the prohibition of cheese of non-Jews also applies to the liquid that remains from the milk after the cheese has curdled, which is called 'whey,' as explained in the note.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":7,"en":"The Mishna (Avoda Zara 29b) relates that R. Yishmael asked R. Yehoshua about the reason for the prohibition of the cheese of non-Jews, and after he tried to answer him briefly and R. Yishmael did not accept it, he diverted him to another subject, because he did not want to answer. The Sages explained that after they enacted a new decree, they did not want to explain its reason for twelve months, so that people would not challenge it. In the Gemara (35b) several reasons are brought, some of which were questioned. In practice, many Rishonim concluded that the reason for the prohibition is the concern that they curdled the cheese with the stomach lining of a nevela, and although the milk is much more than sixty times as much, the stomach lining is not nullified in sixty because it curdles the cheese. So wrote the Rif, Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 3:13; 9:16) and Rosh, and so it was ruled in SA 115:2.<br>The Ra'avad, Rashba and Yam Shel Shlomo (Ḥullin 8:106) hold that even without the reason of a curdling agent the cheese is forbidden, for the Sages wanted to distance Jews from the foods of non-Jews, and since the lining imparts some taste, they forbade the cheese. And it appears that the Rif and those who share his view also agree that the Sages were more stringent regarding the foods of non-Jews than the rules customary for other food prohibitions, for if the prohibition were because of a curdling agent, then according to the rules the cheese should have been forbidden only when it was known that it was curdled with the stomach lining, but when there is a doubt it should have been permitted, since the prohibition of a curdling agent is rabbinic (below 34:6), and in a rabbinic doubt we are lenient. Rather, they decreed a prohibition because of the basic aim of distancing from the foods of non-Jews, and attached the prohibition to a concrete halakhic reason.<br>According to Rabbeinu Ḥananel, the reason for the prohibition is 'nikur' — venom that a snake injected into the cheese, and non-Jews were not accustomed to guard properly against its danger. And this is also the opinion of Rabbeinu Tam (Tosafot Avoda Zara 35a, s.v. \"Lefi\" and \"Ḥada\"), and on this basis he ruled that since this concern no longer exists for generations, the cheese of non-Jews should not be forbidden. And so was the custom in Italy and in Narbonne in southern France, where they curdled the cheese with plants without the stomach lining of a nevela.<br>However, the overwhelming majority of the Rishonim ruled that even when in practice there is no concern of prohibition in the cheese of non-Jews, the cheese is forbidden, since the decree was established in a sweeping manner on all cheeses of non-Jews. So wrote the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 3:14), Ra'avad, Ramban, Rashba (Torat Ha-bayit Ha-katzar 3:6), Ran, Semag and Semak, and so it was ruled in the Tur and SA 115:2. However, the Rema added that in a place where the custom has been to permit since ancient times, they may continue their custom. Today there is no place where the custom is to permit the cheese of non-Jews.<br>Ab initio it is forbidden to curdle cheese even with the lining of a kosher stomach, because of the prohibition of mixing meat and milk. And although by Torah law the prohibition of eating meat and milk applies only when cooked, by rabbinic law it is forbidden even without cooking. However, after the fact, if cheese was curdled with the lining of a kosher stomach, as long as the taste of meat is not perceptible in the cheese, the cheese is kosher (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 9:16; SA 87:11; Responsa Rabbi Akiva Eiger, Mahadura Kamma 207). It is not forbidden like cheese curdled with the stomach lining of a nevela, because a nevela is forbidden in itself, whereas the stomach lining of an animal slaughtered according to halakha is kosher, and the entire prohibition is only because of mixing meat and milk, and as long as the taste of the meat is not perceptible, there is no prohibition in this (Rambam, Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 3:13). The Sages did not decree regarding this, since there is no cooking of meat with milk here, and the concern of prohibition is only rabbinic, and one does not enact a decree on account of a rabbinic concern (Tosafot Ḥullin 116a, s.v. \"Ha-ma'amid\")."},{"n":8,"en":"<b>Whey</b>: During the process of curdling milk into cheese, the casein proteins in the milk bind together into cheese, and the milk liquid becomes thin, like a kind of cloudy water. This liquid is called in the Talmud 'nasyuvei de-ḥalba,' also called 'mei ha-ḥalav' (<i>whey</i>), and it consists of milk sugar (lactose) and whey protein. Some say that the whey of non-Jews is forbidden like milk, lest they mixed the milk of an impure animal into it (Ḥikekei Lev 1:35; Kaf Ha-ḥayim 52; Shevet Ha-levi 4:86). And some say that in a place where there is no concern that impure milk will be mixed into the whey, there is no prohibition in it (Pri Ḥadash 21; Igrot Moshe 3, YD 17). And some hold that whey is not included in the decree of milk of non-Jews (Knesset Ha-gedola, Hagahot Ha-tur 2, in the name of Shiltei Ha-gibborim; Darkhei Teshuva 1).<br>In the food and pharmaceutical industries, proteins extracted from whey or lactose separated from whey by filtration are sometimes used. Some say that their law is like that of whey, which is subject to dispute (Rav Elyashiv). And some say that even those who are stringent regarding whey would agree that they are kosher, for since they have been transformed they are not included in the decree, and in addition, one may rely on government supervision that forbids mixing in impure milk (Ḥelkat Ya'akov; Tzitz Eliezer 17:76:1).<br>The law of whey is like the law of milk, and therefore it renders forbidden meat that is cooked with it (SA 87:6; Shakh 17). Some say that this is a decree because of appearances (mar'it ayin), and therefore it is permitted to eat it after meat, though not actually together with it (Rav Elyashiv, Kovetz Teshuvot YD 73), but it is accepted to regard it as dairy in every respect. However, a medicine intended for swallowing, one of whose components is lactose or milk proteins, is not considered dairy, and it is not forbidden to take it after eating meat (Igrot Moshe 2, YD 26; Shevet Ha-levi 7:118)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-08/","title_he":"ח – היתרי הכנת גבינה על ידי גויים","title_en":"Permitted Ways for Non-Jews to Make Cheese","body_en":"<p>If the milk belongs to a Jew who hired the non-Jew as a worker to prepare cheese from it for him, according to many, as long as it is clear that there is no forbidden admixture in the cheese, the cheese is kosher. For the Sages forbade 'cheese of non-Jews,' meaning cheese that the non-Jew made from his own milk, and not cheese that he made from milk belonging to a Jew (Shakh 115:20; Pri Ḥadash 15; Noda Bi-Yehuda).</p>\n<p>According to most poskim, just as when a Jew saw the non-Jew milking a pure animal, the milk milked under his supervision is kosher, so too, if a Jew saw that the non-Jew prepared cheese from kosher milk without adding the stomach lining of a nevela to it, the cheese is kosher (Rema 115:2; Minḥat Ya'akov; Noda Bi-Yehuda). And some say that even in this case the cheese is forbidden, and only if the Jew himself places the rennet in the milk to curdle it will the cheese be kosher (Shakh 20).<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"According to Tosafot (Avoda Zara 35a, s.v. \"Lefi\"), if it is known that the non-Jew milked the milk in order to prepare cheese from it, even though a Jew did not see the milking, the cheese is kosher, because the non-Jew knows that impure milk does not curdle, and he is not foolish enough to mix impure milk into milk intended for making cheese, and consequently there is no concern that there is an admixture of impure milk there. And so ruled in practice the Ginat Veradim, Nehar Mitzrayim, Pe'ulat Tzaddik 2:257, and Igrot Moshe YD 3:16. On the other hand, according to Rashi, if a Jew did not see the milking, even though he saw the making of the cheese, the cheese is forbidden because of milk of non-Jews, lest the non-Jew intended to drink the milk and mixed impure milk into it, and afterwards changed his mind and made it into cheese. And so ruled the Knesset Ha-gedola, Taz 115:11, and Shulḥan Gavoha 20. Many concluded that ab initio one should be stringent like Rashi, and in a pressing situation and in a case of great loss one may be lenient like Tosafot (Rema 115:2; Torat Ḥatat 81:2)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-09/","title_he":"ט – גבינות רכות","title_en":"Soft Cheeses","body_en":"<p>The poskim disagree about the law of soft cheeses, such as white cheese, cottage cheese, leben and yogurt, which are formed by natural souring. That is, when milk is placed in a warm place, naturally, through the bacteria in the milk, the milk sours and becomes thick. In the first stage it becomes leben, and after some time it thickens into a soft cheese. To speed up the process of their formation, leftovers of leben or cheese that soured, which contain bacterial cultures, are added; different kinds of starters produce different dairy products, all of which have in common that they did not harden like cheese through enzymes but rather thickened through bacterial souring. Today, various concentrates of select bacteria are grown in advanced laboratories, which sour the milk precisely according to the desired product, and in this way dairy products are produced with a precise texture and taste.</p>\n<p>Some permit the soft cheeses of non-Jews, because the decree on cheese is due to the concern that it is curdled with the stomach lining of a nevela, and consequently it applies only to cheeses curdled by means of enzymes found in the stomach; but soft cheeses, which are not curdled by enzymes but by bacteria, are permitted because they are not included in the prohibition.</p>\n<p>However, according to most poskim, even if we say that the Sages decreed a prohibition on the cheese of non-Jews out of concern that they curdled it with enzymes of the stomach lining of a nevela, the decree applies to all dairy products that have thickened. In addition, some say that cheese was forbidden also out of concern that impure milk was mixed into the pure milk, and the softer the cheese, the greater this concern.</p>\n<p>As a matter of halakha, it is proper to be stringent in accordance with the opinion of most poskim, and those who wish to be lenient may do so. In practice, there are kashrut bodies abroad that are lenient and give certification to soft cheeses made by non-Jews, while supervising that they are made from kosher ingredients. And in the Land of Israel the practice follows the opinion of most poskim, not to give certification to soft cheeses of non-Jews.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The poskim disagree about soft cheeses made by cooking, without curdling by enzymes, such as ricotta cheese, which is made by cooking the milk or the nasyuvei, that is, the whey left over from cheese. And likewise regarding kaymak cheese, which is made by prolonged cooking of milk over a low flame, collecting what congeals on top of the milk, and cooling it. According to the Pri Ḥadash 115:21, since they are not curdled by enzymes, there is no concern that they will be curdled with the stomach lining of a nevela, and there is also no concern that the non-Jew will mix impure milk into them, since it does not congeal and consequently there is no benefit in it. And so Responsa Ḥikrei Lev (YD 139) was lenient regarding yogurt. It is reasonable that the poskim who were lenient regarding one of the kinds of soft cheese would be lenient regarding all the kinds, since the reason for the leniency is the same. Under OU supervision the practice is to certify these cheeses based on Rav Henkin, who permitted them even if a little kosher rennet is used. And Igrot Moshe YD 2:48 inclined to this regarding cottage cheese.<br>On the other hand, according to most poskim all these cheeses are forbidden, since the decree was by a vote and applies even to cheeses about which there is no concern that they will be prepared with the stomach lining of a nevela. So wrote the Ḥokhmat Adam 53:38 and Arukh Ha-shulḥan 115:16. And all the Aḥaronim who were stringent regarding one of the soft cheeses would agree to this, among them: Radbaz vol. 6, §2291; Shiyurei Berakha 115:5, and Ḥayim She'al 1:43; Maharam Ḥagiz, Responsa Halakhot Ketanot 1:1, in the name of Mahariks; Ḥikekei Lev YD 37-38. Regarding cheeses made by cooking, such as ricotta and kaymak, some say that they should also be forbidden because of the prohibition of milk of non-Jews, since they were made by cooking and absorbed the taste of the milk (Shiltei Ha-gibborim, Avoda Zara 16a in the pages of the Rif, letter 2). Just as there is a dispute about cheeses, they also disagreed about kinds of cream, and here too it is proper to be stringent, and one who wishes to be lenient may do so (see 'He-ḥalav U-mutzarav Ve-kashrutam,' p. 224)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-10/","title_he":"י – חמאה","title_en":"Butter","body_en":"<p>Butter is formed from milk by churning, that is, agitating the milk in a circular motion in order to separate the fat particles from the milk into a fatty layer, which is kneaded, compressed and consolidated into butter. Since the time of the Ge'onim the customs have been divided: some forbade the butter of non-Jews and some permitted it.</p>\n<p>The reason of those who forbid is the prohibition of milk of non-Jews, that is, the concern that the non-Jew mixed impure milk into his milk before deciding to make butter from it. And although impure milk does not consolidate into butter, there is concern that the moisture remaining on the butter is from the impure milk. And although the likelihood of this is very low, the prohibition remains in force, because the Sages wanted to distance Jews from the foods of non-Jews (the custom of the Land of Israel in the time of the Ge'onim, Ra'avad and Rashba; Noda Bi-Yehuda Tinyana YD 65-66; Tzemaḥ Tzedek of Lubavitch YD 75).</p>\n<p>The reason of those who permit is that the prohibition of milk of non-Jews does not apply to butter, since impure milk does not consolidate into butter. And the reason they forbade the cheese of non-Jews even though impure milk does not curdle is that they were concerned that it was curdled with the stomach lining of a nevela; but butter, which does not require such curdling, is not forbidden (Pri Ḥadash and the Gra in explaining the Rambam).<sup>11</sup></p>\n<p>According to the Rambam (Hilkhot Ma'akhalot Asurot 3:16), if the butter is cooked until the remnants of the milky liquid evaporate, there is no longer reason for concern according to those who forbid, for if there were remnants of impure milk on the butter, in the cooking they would evaporate or be completely nullified in the butter. And this is the opinion of most poskim (SA and Rema 115:3).</p>\n<p>However, sometimes, for various reasons, the rabbis forbade butter. For example, in places where non-Jews cooked the butter in vessels that contained remnants of forbidden foods (Issur Ve-heter He-arokh 48:3), places where they mixed impure milk of a female camel into the butter (Pri To'ar 115:8), and places where they mixed forbidden fat (ḥelev) or lard into the butter (Ḥokhmat Adam 67:9; Arukh Ha-shulḥan 115:27).</p>\n<p>In practice, as long as it is clear that the butter contains no forbidden ingredients, it is permitted, for the rule in a dispute concerning rabbinic law is that the halakha follows the lenient opinion, all the more so when this is the opinion of most poskim. And so most kashrut bodies practice, permitting the butter of non-Jews after checking that all the ingredients from which it is made are kosher. Those who are scrupulous take care to buy butter that was produced by Jews or whose production a Jew supervised.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"And some say that the basis of the prohibition of milk and cheese is the concern that a snake injected venom into the milk, and although today there are no snakes among us, the prohibition that the Sages decreed on milk and cheese remains in force; but what they did not decree upon, such as butter, should not be included in the prohibition (Sha'arei Dura, Rabbeinu Peretz)."}]},{"slug":"17-30-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-11/","title_he":"יא – חלב שנחלב בשבת באיסור","title_en":"Milk Milked Forbiddenly on Shabbat","body_en":"<p>Milking on Shabbat is forbidden by Torah law (Shabbat 95a). However, to prevent suffering to the cows, for if they are not milked they will suffer greatly, it is permitted to ask a non-Jew to milk them. And although the Sages forbade a Jew to ask a non-Jew to do melakha for him on Shabbat, because of 'the suffering of animals' (tza'ar ba'alei ḥayim) the Sages set aside their own enactment. That milk is muktzeh for the whole of that Shabbat, and after Shabbat it is permitted to drink it or sell it (SA OḤ 305:20). Today the dairy industry has advanced, and milking is done by a pumping machine, and on Shabbat one should milk by means of 'gerama' (indirect causation). That is, the 'gerama' mechanism of the machine is activated on Friday, and on Shabbat a Jew attaches the milking cups to the cows' udders by vacuum, and this attachment causes the machine to begin pumping after a short time. This 'gerama' is permitted in order to prevent suffering to the cows, and after Shabbat it is permitted to benefit from the milk, since it was milked in a permitted manner. It is also possible to ask a non-Jew to milk on Shabbat using the pumping machine in the way one milks on a weekday, and after Shabbat it will be permitted to benefit from the milk (Peninei Halakha: Shabbat 20:4).</p>\n<p>But if the milk was milked by a Jew in desecration of Shabbat, by rabbinic law the milk is forbidden to the milker and to anyone for whom it was milked, so that they do not benefit from something done in desecration of Shabbat. And when it is milk intended to be marketed to the general public, it is forbidden to the entire public. In practice, however, since milk milked in desecration of Shabbat is mixed with milk milked on other days and with milk milked on Shabbat by a non-Jew, regarding any milk that reaches the store there is a doubt whether it was milked in a forbidden manner; and since the prohibition of benefiting from something done in desecration of Shabbat is rabbinic, in a situation of doubt there is no prohibition, and kashrut certification may be given to milk and dairy products that may contain milk milked in desecration of Shabbat. However, one should prefer milk from farms where Shabbat observance is maintained, both because it is kosher without doubt, and because it is proper to encourage Shabbat observance and so as not to assist in the desecration of Shabbat (Peninei Halakha: Shabbat 26:7). Therefore, 'mehadrin' kashrut should not be given to dairy products that contain milk milked in desecration of Shabbat.</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"17-30-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/17-30-12/","title_he":"יב – חלב אשה","title_en":"Human Milk","body_en":"<p>A woman's milk, whether she is Jewish or not Jewish, is permitted to eat, provided that the milk has separated from her, for example, if she pumped it into a vessel. And it is not considered milk with respect to the prohibition of meat and milk.</p>\n<p>But the Sages forbade suckling milk from a woman, and only an infant is permitted to suckle milk from his mother or from another wet nurse until he reaches the age of twenty-four months. After he reaches this age, if the child continues to nurse continuously, it is permitted to nurse him until the age of four, and if he is weak, until the age of five. But if he stopped nursing for three days (72 hours), and this interruption was not because he was sick, it is forbidden to nurse him, and his law is like that of any person: he is permitted to consume the milk after it has been pumped, but he is forbidden to suckle from the breast itself (Ketubot 60a; SA YD 81:7).<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":12,"en":"The rule \"Anything that comes from the impure is impure\" (Bekhorot 5b) does not apply to a woman's milk, because only what comes from something forbidden by a Torah negative commandment is forbidden, whereas human flesh is forbidden because of a positive commandment (Rambam) or by rabbinic law (Rosh and Rashba). But the Sages forbade suckling from a woman, lest people err as a result and permit impure milk, saying: just as it is forbidden to eat human flesh, but the milk that comes from a woman is permitted, so too, even though the meat of impure domesticated and wild animals is forbidden to eat, the milk that comes from them is permitted.<br>The milk of a non-Jewish woman is kosher like the milk of a Jewish woman, and so it is stated in Yeshayahu (49:23): \"Kings shall be your nursemaids, and their queens your wet nurses…\" However, some say that if a wet nurse must be found for a Jewish infant, a Jewish wet nurse should be preferred (Ran in the name of the Rashba; Rema 81:7). And if they hired a wet nurse who is not Jewish, it is a custom of piety to ask her to eat kosher foods (Torat Ḥatat 65:11)."}]}]});
